ks  on the companies involved in their cases  but it wasn't the only shocker because  once the wall street journal released  their reports people started to notice  other federal employees doing the same  thing less than a month after the report  was released a request was put in for an  investigation into federal reserve  officials doing the same thing  turns out more than three high-level  people at america's money printer made  suspicious traits during the time the  fed was responding to the pandemic those  officials resigned and had to sell their  stocks no prison time no fine just a  slap on the wrist and the federal judges  well they had more wiggle room to  explain their conflicts away and here's  how you can do it yourself  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  step number one become a judge step  number two specialize in court cases  dealing with public companies these are  the cases that matter where your  decisions have the power to move stock  prices and if the company is big enough  your decision can move markets step  number three offshore your insider  trading once you know what direction the  case is headed in what direction you're  going to rule in then it's time to  offshore your insider trading your goal  is to create a wall between you and your  trades and there are two ways you can do  that one way is having your investments  managed by your brokers another way is  to do what the politicians do and have  your family members do the trading for  you that way in both cases if you ever  got caught you can just throw your hands  up in the air and say i didn't do those  trades they did i didn't tell them  anything  when you and i both know the truth  [Music]  now you may be wondering how is any of  this possible how is it legal for judges  and officials to trace stocks in the  companies they oversee well technically  it's not in the case of federal judges  there are very specific guidelines for  what to do in a case where there's a  conflict of interest the most important  thing is that you recuse yourself from  the case this means they excuse  themselves from that case and hand it  over to another judge courts have  conflict screening software that stands  for possible conflicts of interest  between the judge and their case if  anything pops up they have to recuse  themselves the only problem is this  system has a lot of holes in it like the  case with judge edgardo ramos the  conflict screening software didn't  recognize that exxon mobil and exxon  mobil oil are the same entity because it  only looks for exact matches  there's also the issue of using brokers  to manage their investments which is  what a lot of judges do in these cases  there's a lot of possible deniability  how was my broker supposed to know that  i was ruling on a case involving  facebook that kind of thing all of this  makes it very easy for judges to  overlook the fact that they're involved  in a massive conflict of interest and  when they get caught they just say they  never knew and they're really sorry the  same goes for federal reserve officials  they have such wide reaching influence  that it's like giving politicians the  keys to the ballot boxes and assuming  they won't be meddling in the election  but back to the original question how is  any of this actually possible it's  because of a very simple rule any  federal judge or official should not  engage in traits or behavior that even  just appear improper they're supposed to  behave in ways that make sure the public  trust them and that leaves a lot up to  interpretation with the rules very  subject to interpretation the judges and  officials were free to do whatever they  want but hopefully that's all about the  change  new legislation is on the table to make  insider training for judges and fed  officials illegal the newly proposed  laws would make it so that federal  judges will have to disclose their  traits online within 45 days of making  them basically the exact same world  congress 